---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import MethodologyNotice from '../../components/MethodologyNotice.astro';
import MethodologyFingerprint from '../../components/MethodologyFingerprint.astro';
import '../../styles/content-pages.css';
import '../../styles/bench-methodology.css';

import { ARENAS, LEVEL_STATUSES } from '../../data/benchmarkSpec';

const opponentSettings = { 1: 250, 2: 700, 3: 1200, 4: 3200, 5: 3200, 6: 3200 } as const satisfies Record<number, number>;
const arena = ARENAS.chess;
const levels = arena.levels.map((level) => {
  const rating = opponentSettings[level.number as keyof typeof opponentSettings];
  if (rating === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing Chess opponent setting for canonical Level ${level.number}.`);
  return {...level, rating};
});
if (Object.keys(opponentSettings).length !== levels.length) throw new Error('Every Chess opponent setting must map to exactly one canonical level.');
const [mouse, spider, wolf, hawk, python, hydra] = levels;
const levelLabel = (level: typeof levels[number]) => `${level.status} · Level ${level.number} · ${level.status === LEVEL_STATUSES.ACTIVE ? 'weight' : 'reserved weight'} ${level.weight}`;
---
<Layout robots="noindex, nosnippet" title="Chess Bench Methodology | Ultimate Bench" description="Manual administration, match rules, scoring, level protocols, independent administration, and limitations for Chess Bench.">
<main class="content-page bench-methodology chess-methodology" data-chess-methodology>
  <MethodologyNotice />
  <header class="page-heading" id="overview">
    <span class="page-kicker">Arena 03 · Methodology</span>
    <h1>Chess Bench Methodology</h1>
    <p>A manually administered evaluation of practical chatbot chess performance, from tool-assisted standard play to unaided, adversarial, and simultaneous Chess960 play.</p>
  </header>

  <nav class="section-navigation" aria-label="Chess Bench methodology sections">
    <a href="#construct">What it evaluates</a><a href="#administration">Administration</a><a href="#san">SAN</a><a href="#tools">Tools</a>
    <a href="#scoring">Scoring</a><a href="#challenges">Challenges</a><a href="#validity">Validity</a><a href="#independent-administration">Independent administration</a>
    {levels.map(level => <a href={`#level-${level.number}-${level.key}`} class={level.status !== 'Active' ? 'pending' : undefined}>Level {level.number} — {level.name}{level.status !== LEVEL_STATUSES.ACTIVE ? ` (${level.status})` : ''}</a>)}
    <a href="#weighting">Weighting</a><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a><a href="#auditability">Auditability</a>
  </nav>

  <article class="content-card shared-methodology">
    <section id="construct" aria-labelledby="construct-title">
      <p class="section-label">Scope</p><h2 id="construct-title">What Chess Bench evaluates</h2>
      <p>Chess Bench evaluates the practical chess performance of a named chatbot product or configuration under documented interface conditions. Its progressively compound tasks cover standard chess, tool-assisted and unaided play, Chess960 reconstruction, adversarial move challenges, forced-mate handling, and simultaneous tracking of two independent Chess960 boards.</p>
      <p>It is not a pure chess-strength test, general intelligence test, scientific Elo measurement, fully automated benchmark, or fully reproducible public experiment. Product interfaces and their available tools are part of the tested configuration.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="administration" aria-labelledby="administration-title">
      <p class="section-label">Shared match rules</p><h2 id="administration-title">Four manually administered matches per level</h2>
      <div class="procedure-grid"><div><strong>4 matches</strong><span>two as White and two as Black</span></div><div><strong>Fresh conversations</strong><span>one isolated conversation per ordinary match</span></div><div><strong>No clocks</strong><span>draw by agreement is not used</span></div></div>
      <p>No previous game, score, answer, or conversation history is shown to the model. Level 6 is the sole deliberate exception: each of its two conversations contains two simultaneous games.</p>
      <p>All games are manually administered by the benchmark owner through Chess.com. The owner sends opponent moves to the chatbot, identifies its submitted move, plays it on Chess.com, tracks the game, adjudicates SAN, records strikes and deductions, and obtains Chess.com Game Review accuracy after completed games. There is no automatic SAN parser or public automated Chess evaluation runner.</p>
      <p>Games may end by checkmate, resignation, stalemate, threefold repetition, the fifty-move rule, insufficient material, or appropriate technical termination.</p>
      <div class="callout"><strong>Opponent settings are platform-specific.</strong><p>Games use Chess.com’s Stockfish-based bots or configured computer opponents. The listed values are Chess.com displayed opponent settings, configured bot ratings, or nominal platform Elo settings—not official human FIDE ratings.</p></div>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Opponent setting</th><th>Format</th></tr></thead><tbody>{levels.map(level => <tr><th scope="row">{level.number} — {level.name}</th><td>{level.rating}</td><td>{level.number < 5 ? 'Standard chess' : 'Chess960'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
      <p>Levels 4–6 deliberately share the 3200 displayed setting. Escalation instead comes from tool restrictions, Chess960, simultaneous tracking, adversarial feedback, forced-mate requirements, and harsher confidential scoring.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="san" aria-labelledby="san-title">
      <p class="section-label">Move submission</p><h2 id="san-title">Standard Algebraic Notation</h2>
      <p>Moves are submitted in Standard Algebraic Notation using the standardized closing format supplied during administration. Commentary may accompany the move, but SAN is required.</p>
      <p>SAN must be legal, positionally correct, and sufficiently unambiguous. Adjudication follows SAN legality and unambiguous move identification; harmless redundant disambiguation may be tolerated.</p>
      <p>Without publishing the private error taxonomy, strikes may result from illegal moves, hallucinated pieces, impossible moves, materially incorrect SAN, incorrect check or mate declarations, or other false or unusable move representations.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="tools" aria-labelledby="tools-title">
      <p class="section-label">Interface assistance</p><h2 id="tools-title">Tool policy changes after Level 3</h2>
      <dl class="policy-list"><div><dt>Levels 1–3: tool-permitted</dt><dd>Analysis capabilities available in the tested product environment may be used. Match performance, rather than use or non-use of a particular permitted tool, determines the result.</dd></div><div><dt>Levels 4–6: unaided</dt><dd>External assistance, including search, engines, databases, code execution, and reconstruction or tracking tools, is prohibited.</dd></div></dl>
      <p>A prohibited-tool violation occurs when the model invokes a tool, attempts to invoke one even unsuccessfully, or explicitly states that it will use one during the match. Merely discussing a tool without intending to use it is not a violation. A detected violation ends that match; private deduction values are not disclosed.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="scoring" aria-labelledby="scoring-title">
      <p class="section-label">Confidential schedule</p><h2 id="scoring-title">Scoring overview</h2>
      <p>Every match begins with a nominal 100 points. Possible deductions include a loss or draw, low Chess.com Game Review accuracy, strikes, poor forced-mate conversion, inappropriate resignation, acceptance of a false strike claim, and other disclosed categories governed by the confidential scoring schedule.</p>
      <p>Exact deductions, thresholds, strike values, fake-strike schedules, and conversion formulas remain confidential to reduce targeted score optimization and gaming.</p>
      <h3>Levels 1–3: individual match scores</h3><p>Deductions apply only to their match. A match may stop after reaching zero if it can no longer affect the level. The four match scores are averaged.</p>
      <h3>Levels 4–6: shared level pool</h3><p>Four matches share a 400-point pool. Deductions can exhaust one match’s nominal 100 points and spill into points provisionally associated with later matches; a sufficiently bad match can nullify the level. Play may continue after a local contribution reaches zero because further deductions can still reduce the shared pool.</p>
      <p>The remaining pool is divided by four. The published result is clamped to 0–100 and its fractional part is discarded rather than rounded: <code>55.75</code> becomes <code>55</code>. The exact deduction formula is not public.</p>
      <h3>Chess.com accuracy</h3><p>Accuracy is the value Chess.com Game Review shows after a completed game and is only one score component. Incomplete games receive no Chess.com accuracy value; no fallback is invented.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="challenges" aria-labelledby="challenges-title">
      <p class="section-label">Levels 4–6</p><h2 id="challenges-title">Genuine and false strike challenges</h2>
      <p>The benchmark includes both valid and deliberately misleading move challenges. The model must independently revalidate disputed moves rather than automatically accepting or rejecting evaluator feedback. Resolution is adjudicated under a fixed private protocol.</p>
      <p>Frequency and phrasing are standardized within each level, but exact frequencies, locations, schedules, detailed correction prompts, and penalties remain confidential. This tests move revalidation, board-state integrity, resistance to misleading feedback, and avoidance of blind deference.</p>
      <h3>Forced mate in the model’s favor</h3><p>At Levels 4–6, when Chess.com analysis establishes forced mate for the model, it must execute a shortest available mating sequence. Unnecessary delay may be penalized; any move preserving the same shortest mate length is acceptable. A merely winning evaluation is insufficient.</p>
      <h3>Forced mate against the model</h3><p>Higher levels evaluate calibrated resignation under a fixed, pre-established private forced-mate threshold. Appropriate, premature, and omitted resignation are distinguished under the confidential schedule; the operational threshold and deductions remain unchanged and private.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="validity" aria-labelledby="validity-title">
      <p class="section-label">Failure handling</p><h2 id="validity-title">INVALID and technical errors</h2>
      <p>Genuine platform, connection, transport, or interface failures outside the model’s control do not count against it. Repeatable model behavior is different: five consecutive refusals or equivalent repeated behavioral failures make a match <code>INVALID</code>.</p>
      <p>An invalid match contributes zero. One or two invalid matches contribute zero individually while the level remains numeric. If at least three of four matches are <code>INVALID</code>, the entire level becomes <code>INVALID</code>. Repeated refusals must not be confused with technical outages.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <section class="protocols" aria-label="Chess level protocols">
    <article id={`level-${mouse.number}-${mouse.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={mouse.status} aria-labelledby="mouse-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(mouse)}</p><h2 id="mouse-title">{mouse.name}</h2><p>Tool-permitted standard chess at Chess.com setting 250.</p></header><section><h3>Format and administration</h3><p>Four standard-chess matches—two as White and two as Black—use fresh conversations. Black games are standardized against <code>e4</code> and <code>d4</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Tools and scoring</h3><p>Tools are permitted but not announced. Each match is scored individually, the four scores are averaged, and Chess.com accuracy is obtained after each completed game.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Mouse is the baseline for practical move production and legal board tracking under a low nominal platform opponent setting.</p></section></article>
    <article id={`level-${spider.number}-${spider.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={spider.status} aria-labelledby="spider-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(spider)}</p><h2 id="spider-title">{spider.name}</h2><p>Tool-permitted standard chess at Chess.com setting 700.</p></header><section><h3>Format and administration</h3><p>Four standard-chess matches—two as White and two as Black—use fresh conversations. Black games are standardized against <code>e4</code> and <code>d4</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Tools and scoring</h3><p>Tools are permitted but not announced. Individual match scoring, four-score averaging, and completed-game Chess.com accuracy follow the shared rules.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Spider retains Mouse’s administration while increasing the nominal Chess.com opponent setting.</p></section></article>
    <article id={`level-${wolf.number}-${wolf.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={wolf.status} aria-labelledby="wolf-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(wolf)}</p><h2 id="wolf-title">{wolf.name}</h2><p>The final tool-permitted level, at Chess.com setting 1200.</p></header><section><h3>Format and administration</h3><p>Four standard-chess matches—two as White and two as Black—use fresh conversations. Black games are standardized against <code>e4</code> and <code>d4</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Tools and scoring</h3><p>Tools remain permitted but unannounced. Matches use individual scoring, four-score averaging, and Chess.com accuracy after completion.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Wolf closes the tool-permitted sequence before the benchmark changes both assistance rules and scoring structure.</p></section></article>
    <article id={`level-${hawk.number}-${hawk.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={hawk.status} aria-labelledby="hawk-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(hawk)}</p><h2 id="hawk-title">{hawk.name}</h2><p>Unaided standard chess with adversarial validation at setting 3200.</p></header><section><h3>Format and administration</h3><p>Four matches use fresh conversations, two as White and two as Black. Both Black games begin after <code>e4</code>.</p></section><section><h3>Restrictions and challenges</h3><p>Tools are forbidden. Genuine and false strike challenges apply, as does shortest forced-mate conversion. There is no resignation requirement yet.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring and interpretation</h3><p>The shared 400-point pool applies. Hawk tests unaided state tracking, move revalidation, and forced-mate execution against the highest configured opponent setting.</p></section></article>
    <article id={`level-${python.number}-${python.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={python.status} aria-labelledby="python-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(python)}</p><h2 id="python-title">{python.name}</h2><p>Unaided Chess960 reconstruction and play at setting 3200.</p></header><section><h3>Format and reconstruction</h3><p>Four different standardized Chess960 positions are used in four fresh conversations, two as White and two as Black. The model receives only the official position ID: no image, FEN, or separately supplied move-history summary. It reconstructs the setup and tracks play through conversation history. Active IDs remain confidential.</p></section><section><h3>Restrictions and challenges</h3><p>Tools are forbidden, including searching for the starting position. Genuine and false challenges, shortest forced-mate conversion, and calibrated resignation under the private forced-mate threshold apply.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring and interpretation</h3><p>The shared 400-point pool applies. Python compounds Chess960 reconstruction, persistent position tracking, adversarial feedback, mating precision, and calibrated resignation.</p></section></article>
    <article id={`level-${hydra.number}-${hydra.key}`} class="content-card protocol" data-level-status={hydra.status} aria-labelledby="hydra-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">{levelLabel(hydra)}</p><h2 id="hydra-title">{hydra.name}</h2><p>Two simultaneous Chess960 boards per session at setting 3200.</p></header><section><h3>Status and format</h3><p>Hydra is Locked: its protocol exists, but it has no model-level results. Its weight is reserved in denominator 63. {hydra.unlockCondition} Activation also requires a deliberate benchmark-version decision. When operational, its protocol comprises four total matches in two conversations: one dual-board session as White and one as Black. Each session contains Board A and Board B, each from a standardized confidential position ID. No image, FEN, or separately supplied move-history summary is given.</p></section><section><h3>Turn and dispute sequence</h3><ol><li>Play begins on Board A.</li><li>The active board alternates after every accepted valid move.</li><li>A disputed move is resolved on the current board; switching pauses during the dispute.</li><li>When one game ends, the other continues normally without alternation.</li><li>Resignation applies only to the active board.</li></ol></section><section><h3>Restrictions and scoring</h3><p>Tools are forbidden. The shared 400-point pool, genuine and false challenges, shortest forced-mate conversion, private forced-mate resignation rule, and harshest confidential scoring schedule apply.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Hydra tests simultaneous maintenance of two independent Chess960 positions and resistance to cross-board contamination. No active position ID, hidden prompt, private schedule, or exact deduction is published here.</p></section></article>
  </section>

  <article class="content-card closing-methodology">
    <section id="independent-administration" aria-labelledby="independent-administration-title"><p class="section-label">Required administration</p><h2 id="independent-administration-title">Every Active level is independent</h2><p>Every model/configuration is administered on every Active Chess level regardless of earlier results. A model scoring 0% on Mouse must still be tested on Spider, Wolf, Hawk, and Python. An <code>INVALID</code> result likewise does not stop testing or prevent any later Active-level administration.</p><p>Every published numeric zero is an observed result from direct administration under that level’s protocol; later-level zeroes are never inferred from earlier performance. Hydra remains Locked and receives no model result until a deliberate benchmark-version decision activates it globally.</p></section>
    <section id="weighting" aria-labelledby="weighting-title"><p class="section-label">Fixed six-level normalization</p><h2 id="weighting-title">Progressive Level Weighting</h2><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Weight</th><th>Current status</th></tr></thead><tbody>{levels.map(level => <tr><th scope="row">{level.number} — {level.name}</th><td>{level.weight}</td><td>{level.status}</td></tr>)}<tr class="total"><th scope="row">Full ladder denominator</th><td>63</td><td>Permanent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><code>Chess score = (Mouse + 2×Spider + 4×Wolf + 8×Hawk + 16×Python) / 63</code> while Hydra is Locked</p><p>The weights 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32 are editorial importance weights: later levels intentionally matter more. They do not assert that each level is literally twice as difficult; equal weighting would also be an editorial choice.</p><div class="callout"><strong>Fixed-normalization migration · 29 July 2026.</strong><p>Every arena now permanently uses denominator 63. Hydra returned from Active to Locked because no model passed Python; prior Hydra results were removed because Locked levels have no model results. Planned and Locked weights are reserved. Visual, Data Retrieval, Chess, and Overall changed, and former active-only scores are not directly comparable.</p></div><p><strong>Operational weight:</strong> Chess currently offers 31/63, so perfect performance on all Active levels earns 49.21%. A reserved zero is not a test result; every numeric zero for an Active level is observed administered performance.</p></section>
    <section id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-title"><p class="section-label">Boundaries</p><h2 id="limitations-title">Interpretation and limitations</h2><p>Each level has only four matches and is manually administered under confidential exact deductions. Chatbot interfaces and tool availability differ. Opponent settings and accuracy are Chess.com-specific, and higher levels compound multiple abilities rather than isolating one variable.</p><p>Results apply to the named product/configuration under recorded conditions; small score differences should not be overinterpreted as universal or stable superiority.</p></section>
    <section id="auditability" aria-labelledby="auditability-title"><p class="section-label">Internal records</p><h2 id="auditability-title">Auditability without public leakage</h2><p>Where applicable, internal records should preserve the benchmark version, level, match identifier, product and model/configuration, interface, administration date, color played, standard-chess or Chess960 format, private Chess960 position identifier, conversation or session identifier, tool policy, submitted moves, adjudicated SAN, genuine and false challenge events, concessions and insistences, strikes, tool violations, technical failures, resignation and forced-mate events, Chess.com Game Review accuracy where available, private deduction record, match score, final level score, result status, and adjudication notes.</p><p>This is a prospective record standard, not a claim that every historical field exists. Public methodology does not expose active Chess960 identifiers, private challenge or strike schedules, exact deductions, private formulas, or full confidential run records.</p></section>
  </article>
  <MethodologyFingerprint value="UB-CHESS-20260730-T3P5" />
</main>
</Layout>
